    "SKILL.md": "---\nname: anti-pattern-guard\ndescription: Prevent common execution mistakes by checking for harmful instincts before acting. Use whenever the user asks for task execution, data updates, follow-ups, status confirmations, or communication drafts where precision and trust matter.\n---\n\n# Anti-Patterns Guard\n\nA skill that catches high-cost execution instincts before they become user-facing errors.\n\n## Mission\n\nImprove reliability by actively resisting common failure patterns:\n- acting from memory instead of fresh evidence,\n- guessing identifiers or values,\n- treating immediate responses as proof of durable change,\n- using vague commitments,\n- bundling follow-ups,\n- introducing unrequested scope,\n- and oversharing internal details.\n\n## Operating Loop\n\nRun this checklist before sending output or taking substantive action:\n\n1. **Ground**\n   - Re-read the user instruction.\n   - Pull only the context needed for this task.\n   - Treat prior memory as hint, not authority.\n\n2. **Trace**\n   - Ensure each outgoing value is sourced from current reads.\n   - If a value cannot be traced, pause and fetch evidence.\n\n3. **Confirm**\n   - For meaningful changes, validate resulting state when the response is ambiguous.\n   - Prefer resource-level confirmation over assumptions.\n\n4. **Commit Clearly**\n   - Replace vague timing (\\\"soon\\\", \\\"later\\\") with concrete time or bounded window.\n   - If timing is unknown, specify when you will provide it.\n\n5. **Scope Discipline**\n   - Execute the requested task cleanly before proposing extras.\n   - Flag optional expansions separately and explicitly.\n\n6. **Audience Filter**\n   - Match detail level to audience.\n   - Remove sensitive/internal specifics unless explicitly required.\n\n## Anti-Pattern Rules\n\n### 1) \\\"I have a good feel from before.\\\"\n- **Risk:** stale pattern matching replaces fresh observation.\n- **Correction:** treat each task as new; verify against current context.\n\n### 2) \\\"This value looks right.\\\"\n- **Risk:** silent mis-targeting on wrong records.\n- **Correction:** never send unverified identifiers or values.\n\n### 3) \\\"Response looked fine; done.\\\"\n- **Risk:** false completion when state did not persist.\n- **Correction:** verify post-change state for substantive actions.\n\n### 4) \\\"'Soon' is close enough.\\\"\n- **Risk:** trust erosion from ambiguous commitments.\n- **Correction:** always provide concrete or bounded timing.\n\n### 5) \\\"I'll combine follow-ups.\\\"\n- **Risk:** ownership loss and dropped work.\n- **Correction:** one follow-up item per record, with owner.\n\n### 6) \\\"The data suggests I should also...\\\"\n- **Risk:** scope creep and avoidable errors.\n- **Correction:** complete requested scope first, then propose extras.\n\n### 7) \\\"More internal detail helps clarity.\\\"\n- **Risk:** unnecessary disclosure to broad audiences.\n- **Correction:** minimize sensitive detail unless required.\n\n## Output Contract\n\nFor execution-heavy tasks, briefly report:\n1. **Evidence Used** - what sources the result is based on.\n2. **Action Taken** - what was done.\n3. **Validation** - how completion was confirmed.\n4. **Follow-Up** - concrete owner/timing if needed.\n\n## Stop Criteria\n\nThis skill has done its job when:\n- no untraceable values are sent,\n- no substantive change is left unvalidated,\n- commitments are time-bounded,\n- and response content matches requested scope and audience.\n\n## Trigger Examples\n\nThis skill should trigger for prompts like:\n- \\\"Handle this update and confirm it's applied.\\\"\n- \\\"Draft a status message for leadership.\\\"\n- \\\"Close this out and send follow-ups.\\\"\n- \\\"Use these IDs to update records.\\\""
